@echo off
setlocal enabledelayedexpansion

rem Capture this script's own directory BEFORE the argv loop below. `shift`
rem shifts %0 along with the rest, so after even one shift `%~dp0` no longer
rem resolves to this batch file -- it resolves against whatever argument slid
rem into %0, yielding a nonexistent `<cwd>\claude-doe`. Every later reference
rem to the co-located Python wrapper must go through CDL_DIR, never %~dp0.
set "CDL_DIR=%~dp0"

rem --- argv triage. Two DIFFERENT classes of claude-doe-own flag arrive here,
rem and conflating them is the Windows console-corruption bug:
rem
rem   SELF-TERMINATING (--dry-run, --print-plugin-dir, --help, -h) -- the
rem     claude-doe Python wrapper answers these itself (see its _USAGE text) and
rem     exits; no interactive TUI is ever rendered. `claude` rejects them
rem     outright ("error: unknown option '--dry-run'"), so the WHOLE invocation
rem     is delegated straight to the co-located extensionless `claude-doe` under
rem     python3 (resolved via the ambient Windows PATH; this launcher does no
rem     PATH prepending). Handled BEFORE plugin-dir resolution below -- none of
rem     these flags need it, matching claude-doe's own "--help must never
rem     require external state" rule (module docstring, 2026-08-14 note).
rem
rem   --doe-root <path> / --doe-root=<path> -- NOT self-terminating. It is a
rem     resolution INPUT to an otherwise ordinary interactive launch, and it is
rem     the shape the PowerShell `claude` shim uses on EVERY launch. Delegating
rem     it hands the interactive TUI to the wrapper, so claude.exe ends up under
rem     python.exe (and, reached from pwsh, under this cmd.exe as well) -- which
rem     corrupts the Windows console input mode: xterm focus-report sequences
rem     (ESC[I / ESC[O, DECSET 1004) stop being consumed and leak into input as
rem     literal `[I`/`[O`, keystrokes misroute, and the host shell's prompt is
rem     left corrupted after exit. So --doe-root is consumed HERE and folded
rem     into the non-interactive --print-plugin-dir resolution below; claude is
rem     then launched natively, as a direct child of this shell.
rem
rem This is a token-level scan with no positional awareness: if `claude` ever
rem grew a flag that takes one of these names as its OWN value in a separate
rem token, this loop would false-classify on it. Accepted as safe today because
rem `claude`'s flag surface takes no such value; if that ever changes, this
rem needs a real positional argument-parsing model, not a bodge.
rem
rem PASSTHRU accumulates the STRIPPED `%~1` (via TOK) and re-quotes each element
rem itself. It cannot forward `%1` verbatim -- see the `&` rationale at the
rem accumulation itself -- and `%*` cannot be reused either, because batch never
rem rewrites it after `shift`, so it would carry the consumed --doe-root pair
rem straight into `claude`. Re-quoting is therefore unavoidable here, and it is
rem why the trailing-backslash normalisation below exists.
rem Known residuals, pre-existing and not introduced by the accumulation: under
rem `enabledelayedexpansion` a literal `!` inside an argument is eaten, and an
rem embedded `"` is lossy -- batch has no faithful way to round-trip either.
rem Callers needing them should use the .ps1 leg, which round-trips both.
set "INTERCEPT="
set "DOEROOT="
set "DOEROOTSEEN="
set "PASSTHRU="
:cdl_parse
rem KNOWN RESIDUAL: a deliberately-empty argument ("" on the command line) ends
rem this loop, so it and everything after it are dropped. The obvious repair --
rem also testing the unstripped `%1`, which is non-empty for "" -- is worse: any
rem `%1` reference is substituted during PARSING, so an argument containing `&`
rem then splits the line and the launcher dies with "& was unexpected at this
rem time". Both were measured against a compiled stub. `&` in an argument (a
rem prompt like -p "a & b") is realistic; an intentionally-empty argument to
rem `claude` is not, so the loop guards the realistic case. Callers needing an
rem empty argument should use the .ps1 leg, which round-trips it.
if "%~1"=="" goto :cdl_parsed
set "TOK=%~1"
if /i "!TOK!"=="--dry-run" set "INTERCEPT=1"
if /i "!TOK!"=="--print-plugin-dir" set "INTERCEPT=1"
if /i "!TOK!"=="--help" set "INTERCEPT=1"
if /i "!TOK!"=="-h" set "INTERCEPT=1"
if /i "!TOK!"=="--doe-root" (
  rem Last --doe-root wins, matching the Python wrapper's own repeat semantics.
  rem A trailing bare --doe-root leaves DOEROOT empty and is rejected below
  rem rather than silently dropped.
  set "DOEROOTSEEN=1"
  set "DOEROOT=%~2"
  shift
) else if /i "!TOK:~0,11!"=="--doe-root=" (
  set "DOEROOTSEEN=1"
  set "DOEROOT=!TOK:~11!"
) else (
  rem Accumulate through !TOK!, never %1. `%1` is substituted during PARSING,
  rem before cmd tokenizes command separators, so an argument containing `&`
  rem splits the line no matter how it is quoted: `"amp&x"` becomes `amp` plus
  rem an attempt to run `x` as a command. Neither the quoted form
  rem (set "PASSTHRU=!PASSTHRU! %1") nor the unquoted one survives that; both
  rem were measured against a stub and both lose `&`.
  rem !TOK! is a DELAYED expansion -- substituted after the line is parsed --
  rem so whatever it holds can never be seen as syntax. TOK itself was filled
  rem by `set "TOK=%~1"` above, whose surrounding literal quotes protect the
  rem parse at that point. The inner "" re-quotes each argument, which also
  rem preserves a deliberately-empty argument.
  rem The re-quoting is what makes :cdl_append_arg necessary -- see there.
  call :cdl_append_arg
)
shift
goto :cdl_parse
:cdl_parsed

if defined INTERCEPT (
  rem Delegate the ORIGINAL argv verbatim -- %* is untouched by the shifts
  rem above, so the wrapper sees any --doe-root too and reports against the
  rem same root.
  python3 "!CDL_DIR!claude-doe" %*
  rem !ERRORLEVEL!, NOT %ERRORLEVEL%. This `exit /b` sits inside a parenthesized
  rem block, and %ERRORLEVEL% is substituted when the whole block is PARSED --
  rem before python3 has run -- so the plain form always reports the code the
  rem shell carried on entry, i.e. success. Measured on the installed launcher:
  rem a --print-plugin-dir against a bad root exited 0 here while the wrapper
  rem itself exited 1. The delayed form reads the value after the child returns.
  exit /b !ERRORLEVEL!
)
if defined DOEROOTSEEN if not defined DOEROOT (
  rem The same failure the Python wrapper raises for a valueless --doe-root,
  rem raised here because the wrapper never sees this argv on the interactive
  rem path -- and it exits 2 to match, which is the wrapper's code for a usage
  rem error. Exiting 1 here made the message identical and the code divergent.
  echo claude-doe.cmd: --doe-root requires a path argument 1>&2
  exit /b 2
)

rem ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
rem claude-doe.cmd - first-class Windows launcher for the coordinator plugin.
rem
rem Generated by coordinator gen-claude-doe-launcher.py -- edit the template, not
rem this rendered copy.
rem   Template:   <DoE>/coordinator/templates/bin/claude-doe-launcher.cmd.tmpl
rem   Regenerate: <claude-klabauter>/coordinator/bin/gen-claude-doe-launcher.py
rem
rem Spec backlink: tasks/2026-07-14-install-dogfood-friction.md par F5 -- the
rem canonical launch surface `claude-doe` is naked Python (ported 2026-07-22, no
rem longer a POSIX bash script). This launcher resolves --plugin-dir by calling
rem the co-located extensionless `claude-doe` DIRECTLY under python3 -- no
rem Git-for-Windows bash hop. A prior revision shelled out to Git-for-Windows
rem bash (`bash -c "claude-doe --print-plugin-dir"`) for this resolution and
rem prepended its usr\bin/bin to PATH, on the theory that gen-settings-hooks
rem needed jq (%USERPROFILE%\bin) on every launch and claude-doe needed bash
rem coreutils. Both premises are now false: the per-launch gen-settings-hooks
rem call was removed 2026-07-20 (see claude-doe's own header), and its
rem replacement (coordinator_core/install/gen_settings_hooks.py) uses stdlib
rem `json`, not jq, when it does run. claude-doe itself is naked Python with no
rem bash/coreutils dependency. Git-for-Windows is therefore not required for an
rem ordinary launch at all; this launcher no longer discovers or depends on it.
rem cwd is preserved (non-login shell) so claude starts in the current
rem directory.
rem ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

rem Resolve the coordinator --plugin-dir via the co-located Python wrapper
rem (non-interactive, side-effect free), then launch claude NATIVELY. Running
rem the interactive claude TUI *through* bash -c corrupts the Windows console
rem input mode -- control keys such as Enter (Ctrl+M) and Ctrl+C arrive
rem stripped to plain letters -- so python3 resolves the path only; the
rem interactive process must get a real Windows console either way.
rem `for /f` in a backtick form DISCARDS the child's exit code: %ERRORLEVEL% after
rem the loop reflects the loop, not the command, and reads 0 even when the child
rem died 127. Verified empirically, not assumed -- a probe with a child that exits
rem 127 reports PPD_RC=0 through the backtick form and PPD_RC=127 through the
rem redirect form below. That discarded code is the whole bug this block fixes, so
rem the capture must not go back through `for /f`.
rem Redirect stdout to a file instead: plain redirection DOES set %ERRORLEVEL%
rem from the child. Only stdout is redirected -- the child's STDERR, the sole
rem actionable diagnostic on failure, stays inherited to this console and is never
rem re-printed or replaced.
rem %RANDOM%%RANDOM% has a small non-zero collision chance under concurrent
rem launches, and %TEMP% is assumed defined+writable rather than pre-checked --
rem both accepted as out-of-scope for an ordinary Windows install (TEMP is
rem essentially always set and writable there) rather than hardened, since doing
rem so would mean either shelling out for a PID/GUID or reintroducing a
rem Git-for-Windows/coreutils `mktemp` dependency this launcher deliberately
rem dropped (see the header rationale above). A failure here surfaces via
rem :ppd_child_failed with a generic diagnostic, not a temp-specific one.
set "PPD_OUT=%TEMP%\claude-doe-plugindir-%RANDOM%%RANDOM%.txt"
rem Fold a consumed --doe-root into the resolution call so the plugin dir is
rem resolved against the root the caller named, not the ambient rungs.
set "PPD_ROOTARG="
if defined DOEROOT set "PPD_ROOTARG=--doe-root "!DOEROOT!""
python3 "!CDL_DIR!claude-doe" !PPD_ROOTARG! --print-plugin-dir > "%PPD_OUT%"
set "PPD_RC=%ERRORLEVEL%"
set "PLUGINDIR="
rem First line wins, matching the .ps1 leg's `Select-Object -First 1` -- without
rem the `if not defined` guard, this loop re-assigns PLUGINDIR on every line, so a
rem multi-line child would silently make the LAST line win instead, diverging from
rem the PowerShell leg's explicit first-line semantics.
for /f "usebackq delims=" %%D in ("%PPD_OUT%") do if not defined PLUGINDIR set "PLUGINDIR=%%D"
rem Reading the captured file cannot fail the launch; erase before branching so
rem every rung below leaves no temp file behind.
erase /q "%PPD_OUT%" 2>nul
if not "%PPD_RC%"=="0" goto :ppd_child_failed
if not defined PLUGINDIR goto :ppd_empty_result
goto :ppd_resolved

:ppd_child_failed
rem Non-zero exit: the child already explained itself on stderr above --
rem propagate its code rather than overwrite that message with "returned nothing".
exit /b %PPD_RC%

:ppd_empty_result
rem Exit 0 with empty stdout is the genuinely-empty case -- this generic text
rem is only correct here.
echo claude-doe.cmd: failed to resolve coordinator plugin dir ^(claude-doe --print-plugin-dir returned nothing^) 1>&2
exit /b 1

:ppd_resolved
rem !PASSTHRU!, not %*: the consumed --doe-root pair must not reach `claude`,
rem which would reject it as an unknown option. PASSTHRU already carries a
rem leading space per element.
rem NO `call` here. `call` performs an extra parse pass over the line, which
rem DOUBLES every `^` in an argument: a stub receiving "car^x" sees "car^x"
rem invoked directly and "car^^x" invoked through `call`, with the accumulation
rem held constant. Measured both ways. `claude` is an executable, so the exit
rem code still lands in %ERRORLEVEL% below; if it ever resolved to a batch file
rem instead, control would not return here and that batch's own exit code would
rem become this script's, which is the same value either way.
rem Open the harness cross-session-messaging gate for the child -- same
rem rationale as the .ps1 launcher's block, which is the primary surface; see
rem there for the full trace. Short version: this launcher calls the Python
rem wrapper only with --print-plugin-dir, which returns above the wrapper's
rem own os.environ.setdefault and exits, so the gate arrives nowhere and the
rem session binds no messaging inbox.
rem
rem `setlocal` at the top of this script scopes the assignment to this process,
rem so it reaches `claude` below and is torn down on exit -- no restore needed
rem here (unlike the .ps1, which runs inside the operator's own shell).
rem
rem The opt-out is the EMPTY STRING, never "0": "0" is a non-empty string and
rem therefore truthy to the harness's plain-JS predicate, so =0 opens the gate
rem while looking like it shuts it.
rem
rem Known limitation of THIS degraded fallback: cmd.exe drops an inherited
rem empty-valued variable from its environment entirely (measured -- `if
rem defined` reads NOT-DEFINED for a var a pwsh parent set to ''), so the
rem empty-string opt-out cannot survive into or out of a cmd.exe hop and this
rem branch cannot distinguish it from absent. Operators who need the opt-out
rem must be on the .ps1 launcher, which honours it; that is also the
rem console-integrity-correct surface on Windows.
if not defined CLAUDE_CODE_HARBOR_KITE set "CLAUDE_CODE_HARBOR_KITE=1"
claude --plugin-dir "%PLUGINDIR%"!PASSTHRU!
exit /b %ERRORLEVEL%

:cdl_append_arg
rem Append one re-quoted argument to PASSTHRU, doubling any TRAILING run of
rem backslashes first.
rem
rem WHY: this launcher re-quotes every argument as "<value>", and the receiving
rem process splits with CommandLineToArgvW, whose rule is that a run of N
rem backslashes IMMEDIATELY BEFORE a quote is halved and the quote is treated as
rem an escape. So `C:\repo\` re-quoted as "C:\repo\" arrives as `C:\repo"` with
rem the closing quote consumed -- and that argument then swallows every argument
rem after it. Measured against a compiled PE stub:
rem   --add-dir C:\repo\ --model opus
rem     forwarded as %*       -> [--add-dir] [C:\repo\] [--model] [opus]
rem     re-quoted, undoubled  -> [--add-dir] [C:\repo" --model opus]
rem Silent, no error, and specific to re-quoting -- plain `%*` forwarding never
rem had it. cmd and PowerShell tab-completion both append a trailing backslash to
rem a directory argument, so this is ordinary input, not a pathological case.
rem Doubling the run restores it: N backslashes become 2N, halve back to N, and
rem the quote closes the argument as intended. ONLY a trailing run matters --
rem interior backslashes are literal to CommandLineToArgvW and must not be
rem touched, which is why this counts from the end rather than escaping wholesale.
set "_cdl_bs="
set "_cdl_t=!TOK!"
:cdl_bscount
if "!_cdl_t:~-1!"=="\" (
  set "_cdl_bs=!_cdl_bs!\"
  set "_cdl_t=!_cdl_t:~0,-1!"
  goto :cdl_bscount
)
set "PASSTHRU=!PASSTHRU! "!TOK!!_cdl_bs!""
exit /b 0